qx|A3|st||rh||ah|Board 3 IMPs   -   August 16, 2024|pn|hondo717,IraCT,danpsych,Sputniks|
md|1SAJ86HK93D98CA975,S9HJ52DAQ64CKQJ82,SQT52HQT4DJ32CT43,SK743HA876DKT75C6|sv|e|
mb|1C|mb|P|mb|P|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I agree with pass even though North has 5 HCP consisting of all quacks (queens and jacks) which are over valued. On the other hand, but as a Kib mentioned that it is always a subject of debate. |
PG| ***** 35 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Many here feel you should never leave partner in 1 of a minor as it could be just 3 cards, but I disagree. Most of the time partner has a 4+ card suit and overbidding as responder will often get you in trouble, particularly when opener has a strong hand.|
PG| ***** 36 ***** |


mb|D|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|East should make a balancing double. It is a perfect hand for it.|
PG| ***** 37 ***** |


mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|2C|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|It is unclear what West should do however. The options are either pass or 2N. 2@C is not a possible bid. That is a cue bid showing an opening hand or close to it and asking East to bid a suit.|
PG| ***** 38 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^^B@2^*BWest: ^*N That is what I meant.|
PG| ***** 39 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^^
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Okay continue although it is not my choice.|
PG| ***** 40 ***** |


mb|P|mb|2H|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Now East could bid any of their 3 suits. You should favor majors so 2@H is a good bid.|
PG| ***** 41 ***** |


mb|P|mb|2N|mb|P|mb|P|mb|P|
pg| ***** 42 ***** |


